course: everyday
lesson: 504
title: A new baby
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A new baby changes the whole family. In Bristol, Ellis, 9, has a new sister, Mabel. Everyone who visits says, «Oh, the baby!» and coos at the cot, and nobody asks Ellis about his day. «She's well boring, she just cries and poos,» he says. He feels jealous and a bit forgotten. That's normal: parents are tired, but they still love him just as much. It helps to say «I miss you» and ask for special time, just him and Mum or Dad. In Kent, when Mum tells Marnie a baby is coming, Marnie asks, «Will you still love me?» Mum says, «More than ever.» Love isn't like cake: it doesn't run out when it's shared.
explain: Babies are hard work, for everyone. In Aberdeen, Rosie's baby brother cries all night and she falls asleep in class. «Late night, Rosie?» «Aye, the bairn was greetin' aw night, miss» («bairn» = baby, «greetin'» = crying, «aw» = all). It gets better as babies grow, and it helps to tell your teacher why you're tired. In Bradford, Hamza hears it's baby number four: «Another one?! We've got no room!» Mum laughs: «There's always room.» Hamza is annoyed and secretly excited. Mixed feelings are normal; you can grumble a bit, then say welcome.
explain: Some babies need extra care. In Leeds, Bryony's baby brother was born early and is in hospital; Mum and Dad are there all the time and Bryony stays with Nan. She hears «intensive care» and doesn't know what it means. It's a special hospital ward where doctors and nurses look after very poorly or very early babies all day and all night. Not knowing is scarier than the truth, so ask your parents what's happening. In New York, Ezra's baby brother has a heart problem and needs an operation; Ezra hears his parents crying. «I'm mad scared,» he tells his friend (New York «mad» = very). Older kids feel the worry too: ask questions and tell a grown-up how you feel.
explain: Sometimes the new baby is in a parent's new family. In London, Teddy, 10, lives with Mum and sees Dad every other weekend. Dad and his partner have a baby, and Teddy sees Dad cuddling her and feels replaced. He isn't: he can tell Dad how he feels, and he is still Dad's kid. Kids find their own ways to cope. In Melbourne, Lola writes a «list of rules» for her baby sister: «No crying. No taking Mum. Share toys.» Her parents laugh and stick it on the fridge.
explain: Finding your own job with the baby helps you bond. In Honolulu, Kalani asks if she can hold her baby brother. Mom says, «Shoots» (Hawaiian slang for «okay, sure») and shows her how to support his head. The baby grabs her finger and won't let go, and Kalani melts. In Dublin, Oisín gets to choose his new sister's middle name: he picks «Saoirse» (said «SEER-sha»). «It's deadly, isn't it?» he tells everyone (Irish slang for «brilliant»). Being involved makes a big brother or sister proud.
fact: When a new baby arrives, older children often feel jealous or left out; this is normal.
fact: Parents of a new baby are tired, but they love their older children just as much.
fact: Asking for special time alone with a parent helps an older child feel remembered.
fact: Love does not run out when it is shared between more children.
fact: New babies often wake the whole family at night; it gets better as they grow.
fact: Mixed feelings about a new baby, annoyed and excited at once, are normal.
fact: Intensive care is a special hospital ward where very poorly or very early babies are watched day and night.
fact: When a baby is ill, older siblings feel the worry too; asking questions and telling a grown-up helps.
fact: When a parent has a baby in a new family, an older child can feel replaced, but he is still that parent's child.
fact: When holding a baby, you must support the baby's head, with a grown-up showing you how.
fact: Helping with the baby, like choosing a name or holding her, helps older kids bond.
fact: «Bairn» is Scots for baby, «greetin'» for crying, «mad» is New York slang for very, «shoots» is Hawaiian slang for okay, and «deadly» is Irish slang for brilliant.
exercise: Visitors coo at baby Mabel and nobody asks Ellis about his day. How does he feel? | Jealous and a bit forgotten | Proud and happy all day long | Bored of the baby and asleep | Angry at the visitors' cars
exercise: What could help Ellis? | Asking for special time with Mum or Dad | Hiding the baby's toys | Ignoring the baby for ever | Telling visitors to go away
exercise: Marnie asks, «Will you still love me?» What does Mum say? | «More than ever» | «A bit less» | «We'll see» | «Only on weekends»
exercise: «Love isn't like cake.» What does it mean? | It doesn't run out when shared | It tastes sweet but makes you sick | You only get one slice of it each | It goes stale if you keep it too long
exercise: «The bairn was greetin' aw night.» What was the baby doing? | Crying all night | Eating all night | Laughing all night | Sleeping all night
exercise: Why does Rosie fall asleep in class? | The baby cried all night | She stayed up gaming | She's poorly with a cold | Class is too boring
exercise: Hamza says «Another one?! We've got no room!» What does Mum say? | «There's always room» | «You'll have to move out» | «You can sleep in the bath» | «We'll get a bigger dog»
exercise: Hamza is annoyed and secretly excited. What does the lesson say? | Mixed feelings are normal | He must pick just one feeling | Being annoyed is bad | Excited means he's fibbing
exercise: What is «intensive care»? | A ward where very poorly babies are watched day and night | A room where babies go to have their first photos taken | A school for babies who were born a bit too early | A shop in the hospital that sells baby clothes and toys
exercise: Bryony doesn't know what's happening. What should she do? | Ask her parents | Guess the worst | Say nothing | Look it up alone at night
exercise: Ezra's baby brother needs a heart operation. What can Ezra do with his worry? | Ask questions and tell a grown-up | Keep it all to himself, every bit | Pretend he hasn't noticed at all | Blame himself for the baby's heart
exercise: «I'm mad scared.» What does «mad» mean in New York slang? | Very | Angry | Crazy | Not
exercise: Teddy sees Dad cuddling his new baby and feels replaced. What is true? | He's still Dad's kid | Dad has a new kid instead | He should stop visiting Dad | Dad doesn't miss him
exercise: What did Lola write for her baby sister? | A list of rules | A birthday card | A shopping list | A secret letter
exercise: What must you support when you hold a baby? | The baby's head | The baby's feet | The baby's toys | The baby's bottle
exercise: Mom says «Shoots». What does it mean? | Okay, sure | Shoot the ball | Go away | Hurry up
exercise: Why is Oisín so proud? | He chose his sister's middle name | He's taller than his new sister | He got a present from the baby | He doesn't have to share his room
test: «new baby = parents love u less, that's just maths». What's true? | Love doesn't run out when shared | Love is shared so each kid gets less | Parents only love the youngest child | The oldest always gets the most love
test: «Ellis is being a brat, it's a baby, get over it». What's the fairer view? | Feeling jealous and forgotten is normal | Jealous kids are bad kids, full stop | Ellis is the only kid who's ever felt this | He should pretend he's happy all the time
test: What would probably happen if Rosie told her teacher the baby keeps her up? | The teacher would understand why she's tired | She'd get a detention for being so tired | The teacher would ring the baby's doctor | Nothing; teachers never care about it
test: «Bryony's nan should keep it all secret so Bryony doesn't worry». What does the lesson say? | Not knowing is scarier than the truth | Kids should never be told anything | Secrets always make children feel safe | Bryony should find out from the internet
test: A cartoon kid sees a new baby and runs away to join the circus. What would really help? | Telling a parent how he feels | Running away like the cartoon | Hiding the baby in a cupboard | Asking for the baby to go back
test: «my dad's got a new baby with his girlfriend, so I don't count any more». What does the lesson say? | You're still his kid, and you can tell him how you feel | You don't count now, so stop going at weekends | The new baby is his real child and you aren't | You should never speak to your dad about it
test: «Lola's rules are so bossy». How did her parents take them? | Laughed and stuck them on the fridge | Sent Lola to her room for the evening | Made the baby sign them at once | Threw the list straight in the bin
test: Kalani's cousin says, «just grab the baby by the arm, it's fine». What's right? | Support the head, with a grown-up showing you | Pick it up by one arm if you're quick about it | Babies are tough, any way of holding is fine | Only hold babies while standing on a chair
test: ★★★ Hamza's family had 3 kids. The new baby makes 4. How many children does Mum now have, and how many brothers and sisters does Hamza have? | 4 children, and Hamza has 3 siblings | 4 children, and Hamza has 4 siblings | 5 children, and Hamza has 4 siblings | 3 children, and Hamza has 3 siblings
test: ★★★ Teddy sees Dad every other weekend. In 8 weeks, how many weekends is he with Dad? | 4 | 8 | 2 | 16
test: «Oisín picked 'Saoirse', that's deadly». What does «deadly» mean here? | Brilliant | Dangerous | Sad | Very old
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the jealousy experienced by an older child upon the arrival of a new baby? | It is a normal feeling | It is evidence of wickedness | It is unique to a few children | It indicates that the parents' love has ceased
exam: State the nature of «intensive care» as described in the lesson. | A hospital ward wherein very poorly or premature infants are observed continuously | An establishment for the instruction of infants born prior to the expected date | A department of the hospital in which infant clothing is sold to visitors | A ward reserved exclusively for the photographing of infants after birth
exam: Identify the course recommended where an older sibling is uninformed as to the condition of a hospitalised infant. | The child should enquire of the parents | The child should assume the gravest outcome | The child should remain silent indefinitely | The child should consult strangers online
exam: It is given that a parent residing apart from a child has had a further child in a new family. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The elder child remains that parent's child, and may express his feelings | The elder child ceases to be regarded as a child of that parent | The elder child is obliged to discontinue all visits forthwith | The elder child must conceal any feelings he may experience
